hello  it is an honor for me to be speaking to  you as part of this very important  symposium  race in america history and  controversies  my focus will be on a key lever that has  been part of a critical foundation for  empowering the black community  to move from persecution to prosperity  that lever is education and the power of  school choice  throughout the struggle for equality of  access and opportunity  the ability for a black parent to choose  a  high quality school for their child  has been the great equalizer for the  black community  but before i share a little bit about  the arduous journey  of the african-american experience of  education in the united states  i'd like to tell you a little bit about  my personal story  in the late 1960s my parents brother and  i  emigrated from jamaica west indies  ultimately moving to  laurelton queens in new york at the time  a predominantly white  middle-class community laurelton was  transitioning from a jewish enclave to  what is now a neighborhood mostly  inhabited by african-american and  caribbean residents  by 1977 i was attending is  231 a junior high school that had been  historically  mostly white but like the surrounding  area  was rapidly becoming racially integrated  racial tensions were growing with  increasingly bitter and daily disputes  driving the school leadership to seek  out solutions  the local school board citing  overcrowding as their rationale  voted to open an annex a separate  academic building two miles away in  rosedale  a small irish and italian american  community  nestled on the border of nassau county  long island  that decision led almost every white  family in my junior high school to  transfer their children  to the annex in september of 1977  leaving is 231 a segregated  virtually all black school my jamaican  immigrant parents  determined that their two sons would get  a real shot at the american dream  were ready to transfer me as well  wherever go the white kids  so goes the quality of the edge of  education was their theory  i was 12 years old and my parents knew  best  but something was not right here i  didn't want to leave my school and i did  not like the reasons my parents were  giving me for doing so  it is a strange and mildly terrifying  thing to consider standing up  to the people you love and up until that  point in my life i had never questioned  my parents about  anything that mattered why would i  question the two people who had  repeatedly demonstrated  that they had only my best interests at  heart  but something inside me said no i found  a voice that i never knew  existed on the sunday evening before  they had to file the transfer documents  i stood in our living room pleading with  my dad and sconced in his brown recliner  and locking eyes with my mom witnessing  her youngest son muster courage  that she had not ever seen i begged my  parents to reconsider  to let me stay in is-231 i remember  thinking to myself and then bravely  saying out loud why should the other  school be better  why should the education suddenly be  worse in my school  just because most of the kids who are  going to be left  are black i like my teachers  and i didn't see why my education would  have to suffer  just because i wasn't going to go to the  school with all the white kids  i told my parents if they let me stay in  is-231  i would work even harder they would not  regret their decision  i promised they could believe in me  because i believed in myself  and regardless of what they were they  were to decide  something had awakened in me to give me  the confidence to respectfully challenge  my parents  i was now an active player in my own  education  and to a larger degree my own destiny  i made a choice i was becoming an  agent of my own uplift  and then something extraordinary  happened my parents relented  they let me stay at is-231  and with the support of my parents and  teachers  i received a public education that made  me believe anything was possible  i ultimately entered brooklyn technical  high school went on to graduate  with a bachelor of science in computer  science engineering at cornell  university  and later earned my mba from harvard  business school  my parents and that choice of education  made all the difference  and now come full circle for the last  decade  i was the ceo of a non-profit network of  public charter schools  in the heart of the south bronx in new  york city hoping to inspire  thousands of low-income primarily black  and hispanic students  to understand the pathways through which  they could become agents of their own  uplift  in a sense the parents of the black and  brown children  i now educated have the same aspirations  that my parents had for me  for many black children and for children  of all races  that education is the ticket to reach  the american dream  but it has not always been so due to the  legacy of slavery  in the mid-1800s black people had very  little access to high quality education  in slave states anti-literacy laws  banned black people from accessing  education  on the premise that a tipping point  of literate slaves might lead to  insurrection and rebellion  for example south carolina had a law  that would  jail a person for six months and impose  crippling fines if you even attempted  to teach a slave to read and write  things did not get much better during  the jim crow era  which legally segregated schools  during that time like much of everyday  life  public schools were segregated by race  they were separate and profoundly  unequal  there were huge disparities in funding  between black and white schools  facilities access to equipment and  most importantly the quality of the  education itself  in 1896 the supreme court  in the case of plessy versus ferguson  ruled  that separate but equal was fair  and was not a violation of the 14th  amendment  requiring equal protection to all  this ruling meant that the federal  government would not  challenge segregation even despite the  deeply  inferior conditions that black students  were subject to  especially in education separate but  equal  and jim crow remained unchallenged  until brown versus board of education in  1954  and the civil rights act of 1964.  it is against this backdrop when racial  segregation was at its worst  that an unlikely partnership transformed  the education  for two generations of black students in  the rural south  educator activist public intellectual  and founder of the tuskegee institute  booker t washington  had himself experienced firsthand  the destitution and profound racial  disparities in the rural south  despite these obstacles washington's  perseverance  and disciplined upbringing eventually  led to an education at what is now known  as hampton university and as an adult  washington dreamed of a school building  project for black communities throughout  the south  that could help begin to lift black  people out of poverty  washington presented his ambitious idea  to build a network of schools  to julius rosenwald who at the time  was a ceo of the nation's largest  retailer  sears rosenwald was a supporter of the  tuskegee institute  so had a deep respectful relationship  with washington  washington collaborated with julius  rosenwald and together they crafted a  plan  to make washington's dream a reality  in a region of the united states in  which jim crow  racial segregation was law lynching was  a common and brutal reality  and rural poverty was widespread on both  sides of the color line  these rosenwald facilities were  typically constructed  maintained and partially funded  by the populations that they served  becoming  centers of local social life and sources  of community pride  over the years to construct the  buildings rosenwald contributed 4.4  million dollars  or what today would be nearly 80 million  dollars  and importantly 4.7 million dollars  came from local black communities in the  form of cash  labor land and materials  washington and rosenwald had launched a  challenge grant  program that ultimately led to the  unprecedented construction  of more than five thousand school  buildings  four thousand nine hundred seventy seven  schools  two hundred seventeen teachers homes  and 163 shop buildings in  15 states from maryland to texas  all dedicated to the education of black  students  while the required donation from african  american communities  amounted to double taxation their  leaders nonetheless  called on residents to organize  fundraising drives  to cover their share of each new school  get busy laurel or hang your heads in  shame  admit admonished the prince george's  colored public school trustees in a 1924  report  describing the existing pitiful  buildings in the town of laurel  these efforts to build or renovate new  school buildings were impressive  in one instance struggling sharecroppers  set aside an area planted with cotton as  the quote  rosenwald patch end quote and donated  the profits from its sale  to the school children saved pennies and  snuff boxes  and at one fundraising rally an old man  who had been a slave offered his life  savings of 38 dollars to quote  see the children of my grandchildren  have a chance end quote  in the years between world war one and  world war ii  the rosenwald schools helped close the  gap between white and black  academic achievement researchers at the  chicago federal reserve  concluded that in the regions where the  rosenwald rural schools initiative  built most of its five thousand schools  the education gap between southern born  black and white males  narrowed sharply in areas such as school  attendance  literacy and cognitive test scores  using census data and world war ii  records  the researchers found that rosen's  rosenwald's program  explained a stunning 40 percent  narrowing of the racial education gap  while the rosenwald schools were  thriving a major case was being  litigated over segregation in 1954.  the supreme court made a landmark ruling  that school segregation was  unconstitutional  in brown versus board of education the  fundamental premise was that quote  separate is inherently  unequal end quote so exclusively black  or white schools were now outlawed  so a decision intended to improve  outcomes for black children  assumed separate had to mean inferior  the terrible irony is that decision  actually had the perverse impact of  making the very  existence of the rosenwald schools a  violation of the law  so even with its extraordinary record of  success  the rosenwald schools were quickly  abandoned  despite student protests over their  closing and demolition  most of the schools were gone by the  1970s  the rosenwald schools remain today one  of the greatest examples of strong  partnership across racial lines  black excellence and ironically the  victim  of unintended consequences fast forward  to today  while progress has been made with more  than three million  black students enrolled in college today  there are still  deep academic racial disparities in  pockets of american society  for example of the nearly 2 000 public  school students beginning high school in  the south bronx  where i led a network of charter schools  in 2015  only 2 percent of kids in this district  graduated ready for college four years  later  a shocking 98 percent of students  either dropped out of high school before  completing their senior year  or they did manage to graduate but would  still be required to take remedial  classes in community college  due to low math and reading scores on  state exams  by contrast charter public schools in  the south bronx  continue to outperform their district  peers in both  math and reading in third in third  through eighth grade by a substantial  margin  it comes as no surprise that parents in  these predominantly  black and hispanic communities  encompassing two of the poorest  congressional districts in the country  are  desperate for higher quality education  options  the new york city charter center reports  that in the bronx alone  more than 25 000 families  applied for just over 9 000 available  seats  in bronx charter schools this  overwhelming demand  demonstrates that parents want the best  for their children  and should be given the power of choice  many of these parents may have faced  racial discrimination and other  challenges  in their own lives and fear that their  children might as well  but they know that a great education  can make a huge difference they do not  believe that their children are doomed  to be shackled  by the horrors of america's legacy of  slavery  they know that if teachers had the  highest of expectations  and were empowered with a curriculum  that could help their children  develop character-based strengths like  integrity resiliency  and agency the belief that you can be  the master of your own destiny  then those children could lead life on  their own  terms even in the face of structural  barriers  that is why i must mention the 1619  project  a major initiative and series of essays  from the new york times magazine  for a year the authors have repeated the  claim that the country's  quote true founding end quote was 1619  and not 1776 when the declaration of  independence was signed  1619 claims that america was founded as  a slaveocracy  not a democracy that anti-black racism  runs in the very dna of the country  the 1619 project also claims that  america's founding ideals were false  when they were written  and that the nation was founded on a lie  despite the fact that the 1619 project  has been discredited by dozens of the  most well-respected historians  in the country particularly the claim  that the civil war was fought to defend  slavery  the new york times was not satisfied  with just the special edition  of the magazine they partnered with the  pulitzer center  to create a curriculum that has now been  adopted in thousands of classrooms  school districts in chicago illinois  washington dc and buffalo new york began  implementing the project  in their k-12 course materials  these are some of the lowest  academically performing  schools in the country  an educator who has spent the last 10  years working with families  who believe and want their child to have  the american dream  the last thing their children need to  hear is  false information about our country yes  america has a terrible history of  slavery and oppression  but america is not defined solely by its  legacy of slavery  we want all children to know of the  legacy of  excellence alongside the legacy of  slavery  and and the lessons that are relevant to  all american children  there are innumerable examples of  individuals  and groups that have gone from  persecution  to prosperity by embracing america's  founding ideals  of family free enterprise faith  hard work and entrepreneurship that is  why i'm  so proud to say that in the same spirit  that booker t  washington demonstrated to envision  how education could be a pathway to  prosperity  how he had an idea to build a network of  schools  throughout the south even in the face of  bitter  and often lethal racial discrimination  well today a group of talented black  scholars and activists  have come together to do the same this  initiative called  1776 unites is led by bob woodson  for 40 years bob woodson developed a  list of 10  principles that he has used in order to  help  local communities transform  struggling neighborhoods and to empower  the people within them  these 10 principles include competence  integrity transparency  resilience witness  innovation inspiration  agency access  and grace these ten  principles now undergird a new  1776 unites curriculum  which will ultimately develop into a  k-12 program  for all schools wherever character  formation is happening  for children every lesson will connect  back to one  or more of the woodson principles  the 1776 unites curriculum offers  authentic inspiring stories from  america's past  and present that show what is best  in our national character and what our  freedom  makes possible even in the most  difficult circumstances 1776 unites  includes two major components  a look back and a look forward  the look back maintains a special focus  on stories  and lesson plans that celebrate black  excellence  reject victimhood culture and showcase  african americans past and present who  have prospered  by embracing americans found america's  founding ideals  of free enterprise family hard work  entrepreneurship and  two examples include biddy mason an  amazing woman who was born a slave  but died a millionaire and a  philanthropist  and elijah mccoy a son of escaped slaves  who became a talented engineer and  inventor  whose products were so superior that  inferior knockoffs were created to the  point that when  engineers went to buy mccoy's products  and were offered the copies  they said no i want the real mccoy  many americans may know that phrase but  do not know it is connected to a black  engineer  the son of slaves these are just two  of the multitude of stories that we will  be that will be  in the look back section of the 1776  unites curriculum  the look forward component includes  units that teach  life lessons that help the next  generation  understand the series of life decisions  that give them the greatest likelihood  of economic success  as they make choices as they make their  passage  into young adulthood despite real racial  barriers that are out there  there are millions of examples of black  excellence  we constantly study poverty what can we  learn  from black americans who've been  successful in pursuing the american  dream  and their decisions around education  work  marriage and children for example  teaching the data  associated with what's called the  success sequence  the data that shows if you finish a high  school degree  secure a full-time job of any kind so  you learn the dignity and discipline of  work  and if you have children marriage first  98 of the people who followed those  steps  avoid poverty and the vast majority end  up in the middle class or beyond  that's the kind of information that  young people of all races need to know  to increase their likelihood of being  economically successful  in the case of my own parents that is  how they achieved the american dream  and made it possible for me to do the  same  i often wonder what would have happened  if my parents had not  acquiesced and instead insisted that i  attend the annex with almost all white  students  would i have exhibited the same level of  ownership over my learning  as i did when i convinced them to grant  me my wish to stay  yet in allowing me to forge a path that  went against their better judgment  my parents taught me a valuable lesson  my  effort could and would have to influence  my outcomes  at the time i felt an enormous added  responsibility  to make my parents decision worth the  trust  that they had placed in me i am  convinced  the feeling that i had on that long ago  sunday evening  the faith i was expressing in myself  and appealing to my parents to  reluctantly  accept was what i now understand  to be my sense of personal agency  that self-belief was the precursor to  the ensuing efforts and actions  necessary for me to fulfill the  commitment that i had made to my parents  and most importantly to myself  but what was also important was the fact  that my parents had the ability  to choose and to chart a path through  education to make life better for me  and by extension the next generation it  is through this type of innovation and  commitment  whether it be booker t washington or bob  woodson or my parents  that young americans of all races can  understand  that even while race has been an issue  in our country  it is actually the power of education  the power of america's founding ideals  and our founding documents signed in  1776  that have within them the tools for  self-renewal  and self-betterment in chapter 13  of de tocqueville's america he said  quote the greatness of america  lies not in being more enlightened than  any other nation but rather in her  ability  to repair her faults end quote  i have always found that statement  compelling  because it resonates with the notion  that america  is always in pursuit of becoming a more  perfect union  while the founders laid out inspiring  ideals  we are in a constant effort to fully  live up to them  that is the critical story about race  and education  because how we make steady progress  is through reminding and embracing  those founding ideals helping our next  generation to do that  and not rejecting them thank you  you

