istricts in chicago illinois  washington dc and buffalo new york began  implementing the project  in their k-12 course materials  these are some of the lowest  academically performing  schools in the country  an educator who has spent the last 10  years working with families  who believe and want their child to have  the american dream  the last thing their children need to  hear is  false information about our country yes  america has a terrible history of  slavery and oppression  but america is not defined solely by its  legacy of slavery  we want all children to know of the  legacy of  excellence alongside the legacy of  slavery  and and the lessons that are relevant to  all american children  there are innumerable examples of  individuals  and groups that have gone from  persecution  to prosperity by embracing america's  founding ideals  of family free enterprise faith  hard work and entrepreneurship that is  why i'm  so proud to say that in the same spirit  that booker t  washington demonstrated to envision  how education could be a pathway to  prosperity  how he had an idea to build a network of  schools  throughout the south even in the face of  bitter  and often lethal racial discrimination  well today a group of talented black  scholars and activists  have come together to do the same this  initiative called  1776 unites is led by bob woodson  for 40 years bob woodson developed a  list of 10  principles that he has used in order to  help  local communities transform  struggling neighborhoods and to empower  the people within them  these 10 principles include competence  integrity transparency  resilience witness  innovation inspiration  agency access  and grace these ten  principles now undergird a new  1776 unites curriculum  which will ultimately develop into a  k-12 program  for all schools wherever character  formation is happening  for children every lesson will connect  back to one  or more of the woodson principles  the 1776 unites curriculum offers  authentic inspiring stories from  america's past  and present that show what is best  in our national character and what our  freedom  makes possible even in the most  difficult circumstances 1776 unites  includes two major components  a look back and a look forward  the look back maintains a special focus  on stories  and lesson plans that celebrate black  excellence  reject victimhood culture and showcase  african americans past and present who  have prospered  by embracing americans found america's  founding ideals  of free enterprise family hard work  entrepreneurship and  two examples include biddy mason an  amazing woman who was born a slave  but died a millionaire and a  philanthropist  and elijah mccoy a son of escaped slaves  who became a talented engineer and  inventor  whose products were so superior that  inferior knockoffs were created to the  point that when  engineers went to buy mccoy's products  and were offered the copies  they said no i want the real mccoy  many americans may know that phrase but  do not know it is connected to a black  engineer  the son of slaves these are just two  of the multitude of stories that we will  be that will be  in the look back section of the 1776  unites curriculum  the look forward component includes  units that teach  life lessons that help the next  generation  understand the series of life decisions  that give them the greatest likelihood  of economic success  as they make choices as they make their  passage  into young adulthood despite real racial  barriers that are out there  there are millions of examples of black  excellence  we constantly study poverty what can we  learn  from black americans who've been  successful in pursuing the american  dream  and their decisions around education  work  marriage and children for example  teaching the data  associated with what's called the  success sequence  the data that shows if you finish a high  school degree  secure a full-time job of any kind so  you learn the dignity and discipline of  work  and if you have children marriage first  98 of the people who followed those  steps  avoid poverty and the vast majority end  up in the middle class or beyond  that's the kind of information that  young people of all races need to know  to increase their likelihood of being  economically successful  in the case of my own parents that is  how they achieved the american dream  and made it possible for me to do the  same  i often wonder what would have happened  if my parents had not  acquiesced and instead insisted that i  attend the annex with almost all white  students  would i have exhibited the same level of  ownership over my learning  as i did when i convinced them to grant  me my wish to stay  yet in allowing me to forge a path that  went against their better judgment  my parents taught me a valuable lesson  my  effort could and would have to influence  my outcomes  at the time i felt an enormous added  responsibility  to make my parents decision worth the  trust  that they had placed in me i am  convinced  the feeling that i had on that long ago  sunday evening  the faith i was expressing in myself  and appealing to my parents to  reluctantly  accept was what i now understand  to be my sense of personal agency  that self-belief was the precursor to  the ensuing efforts and actions  necessary for me to fulfill the  commitment that i had made to my parents  and most importantly to myself  but what was also important was the fact  that my parents had the ability  to choose and to chart a path through  education to make life better for me  and by extension the next generation it  is through this type of innovation and  commitment  whether it be booker t washington or bob  woodson or my parents  that young americans of all races can  understand  that even while race has been an issue  in our country  it is actually the power of education  the power of america's founding ideals  and our founding documents signed in  1776  that have within them the tools for  self-renewal  and self-betterment in chapter 13  of de tocqueville's america he said  quote the greatness of america  lies not in being more enlightened than  any other nation but rather in her  ability  to repair her faults end quote  i have always found that statement  compelling  because it resonates with the notion  that america  is always in pursuit of becoming a more  perfect union  while the founders laid out inspiring  ideals  we are in a constant effort to fully  live up to them  that is the critical story about race  and education  because how we make steady progress  is through reminding and embracing  those founding ideals helping our next  generation to do that  and not rejecting them thank you  you
 

